      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b9H",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1703356144,
      "version": 1671,
      "versionNonce": 1143750672,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1777959593270,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "If a million users simultaneously try to buy, a last pair of shoes, a standard DB locking will fail or crash the system.\nMoving to a single Redis node solves the problem, but creates a massive CPU bottleneck.\nWe need to solve both the atomicity and the CPU bottleneck issue simultaneously. \nAtomic Lua Scripts : \nLua is a lightweight programming language that can be executed atomically using Redis to perform complex operations\nwithout multiple round trips b/w the client and server.\nTo prevent race conditions, we preload the inventory count into Redis. When a checkout request arrives, we execute \nthe Lua script, and because Redis is single threaded, it blocks the other ops while the script runs, guaranteeing that \nthe stock > 0, decrement happen in indivisible unit in microseconds.\n\nAgain, we need to have the inventory sharded. If all requests go to a single node, that execute the Lua script, then\nthis will max out a CPU core and we need to distribute.\nInstead of holding 10,000 items under one key, we can distribute inventory across multiple nodes, mapping to different \nkeys which map to different nodes.\nWe can have deterministic routing -- Order Service can have consistent hashing on the User id -- to route to a particular\nnode on the inventory. Allows multiple Redis cores to run the atomic Lua script to decrement, vastly incrementing the \nthroughput.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "If a million users simultaneously try to buy, a last pair of shoes, a standard DB locking will fail or crash the system.\nMoving to a single Redis node solves the problem, but creates a massive CPU bottleneck.\nWe need to solve both the atomicity and the CPU bottleneck issue simultaneously. \nAtomic Lua Scripts : \nLua is a lightweight programming language that can be executed atomically using Redis to perform complex operations\nwithout multiple round trips b/w the client and server.\nTo prevent race conditions, we preload the inventory count into Redis. When a checkout request arrives, we execute \nthe Lua script, and because Redis is single threaded, it blocks the other ops while the script runs, guaranteeing that \nthe stock > 0, decrement happen in indivisible unit in microseconds.\n\nAgain, we need to have the inventory sharded. If all requests go to a single node, that execute the Lua script, then\nthis will max out a CPU core and we need to distribute.\nInstead of holding 10,000 items under one key, we can distribute inventory across multiple nodes, mapping to different \nkeys which map to different nodes.\nWe can have deterministic routing -- Order Service can have consistent hashing on the User id -- to route to a particular\nnode on the inventory. Allows multiple Redis cores to run the atomic Lua script to decrement, vastly incrementing the \nthroughput.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
