    {
      "id": "H9cXeKm_3ya2NLOr88Auv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 411.78385416666697,
      "y": 3776.7109374999995,
      "width": 1006.2239379882812,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b9Q",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 762763504,
      "version": 1500,
      "versionNonce": 318281232,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1778165015273,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Reserving inventory is only half the battle. Situations can happen where a user closes the browser window, or the payment fails?\nDecrementing the counter in Redis permanently means we lose the item forever.\nWhen the Redis  Lua script decrements the global stock, it simultaneously writes a key with an expiration, like a 5 min TTL.\nWe can then have a Payment Saga.\n1. Our backend worker writes the order to Postgres, in a Pending Payment state.\n2. If payment is successful by external payment gateways, a webhook updates the Postgres DB to confirmed and permanently\nremoves the Redis hold key.\n3. Timeout Recovery for reclaiming stock : \nWe can't rely on Redis keyspace notifications for expiration as they are fire and forget in nature and risk permanent data loss.\nIf a worker crashes, we instead use a background sweeper, a cron, in our expiration workers.\nAfter 5 mins, expiration workers will check the database and if the order is still in Pending payment, it marks the oder as \nCancelled and atomically increments the inventory back up on the exact Redis shard it originated from, thus returning it to \navailable pool, so that it can be purchased by other users.\n\nReservation Hold + Payment SAGA + Timeout Recoveries : Ensure that no stock basically gets claimed but never purchased.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Reserving inventory is only half the battle. Situations can happen where a user closes the browser window, or the payment fails?\nDecrementing the counter in Redis permanently means we lose the item forever.\nWhen the Redis  Lua script decrements the global stock, it simultaneously writes a key with an expiration, like a 5 min TTL.\nWe can then have a Payment Saga.\n1. Our backend worker writes the order to Postgres, in a Pending Payment state.\n2. If payment is successful by external payment gateways, a webhook updates the Postgres DB to confirmed and permanently\nremoves the Redis hold key.\n3. Timeout Recovery for reclaiming stock : \nWe can't rely on Redis keyspace notifications for expiration as they are fire and forget in nature and risk permanent data loss.\nIf a worker crashes, we instead use a background sweeper, a cron, in our expiration workers.\nAfter 5 mins, expiration workers will check the database and if the order is still in Pending payment, it marks the oder as \nCancelled and atomically increments the inventory back up on the exact Redis shard it originated from, thus returning it to \navailable pool, so that it can be purchased by other users.\n\nReservation Hold + Payment SAGA + Timeout Recoveries : Ensure that no stock basically gets claimed but never purchased.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "fs62PTS0QGEMOGNVoFPTI",
